  "helpPairWhatTitle": "What pairing is",
  "@helpPairWhatTitle": {
    "description": "Topic 1 title."
  },
  "helpPairWhatShort": "Pair once with someone; after that, transaction files and invoices between the two wallets are sealed.",
  "@helpPairWhatShort": {
    "description": "Topic 1 lede."
  },
  "helpPairWhatP1": "Pairing links two wallets once: with a request to a contact you have paid over Epicbox, or with your invite, scanned as a QR or opened as a link. Each wallet keeps a key made for that one contact; nothing is shared with anyone else.",
  "@helpPairWhatP1": {
    "description": "Topic 1."
  },
  "helpPairWhatP2": "After pairing, a transaction file to that contact is **sealed**: only their wallet can open it, and it cannot be changed or forged on the way. Payments over Epicbox between people keep working as before.",
  "@helpPairWhatP2": {
    "description": "Topic 1."
  },
  "helpPairWhatStep1": "Open [[Contacts]] and choose **Pair**.",
  "@helpPairWhatStep1": {
    "description": "Topic 1 step."
  },
  "helpPairWhatStep2": "Pair with a contact, or show your invite and let them scan it.",
  "@helpPairWhatStep2": {
    "description": "Topic 1 step."
  },
  "helpPairWhatStep3": "Check the words together (not needed after a request), then **They match**.",
  "@helpPairWhatStep3": {
    "description": "Topic 1 step."
  },
  "helpPairWhatP3": "Pairing needs BT Wallet on both sides. A contact that is not paired still gets ordinary transaction files and Epicbox payments.",
  "@helpPairWhatP3": {
    "description": "Topic 1."
  },
  "helpPairWhyTitle": "Why verify",
  "@helpPairWhyTitle": {
    "description": "Topic 2 title."
  },
  "helpPairWhyShort": "Verifying proves the pairing reached the right wallet and not someone in between.",
  "@helpPairWhyShort": {
    "description": "Topic 2 lede."
  },
  "helpPairWhyP1": "Both wallets show the same four pictures and names after pairing. They come from the two wallets’ keys, so a pairing that someone changed on the way shows different words on the two screens.",
  "@helpPairWhyP1": {
    "description": "Topic 2."
  },
  "helpPairWhyP2": "Compare them on a call or in person, then tap **They match**. The contact becomes **Verified**. **Skip for now** leaves it **Paired, not verified**: transaction files are still sealed, but the contact cannot send invoices until the words are checked.",
  "@helpPairWhyP2": {
    "description": "Topic 2."
  },
  "helpPairWhyP3": "If the words differ, tap **They do not match**. The pairing is dropped on this side. Pair again, in person or with a fresh invite.",
  "@helpPairWhyP3": {
    "description": "Topic 2."
  },
  "helpPairWhyP4": "The word names stay in English in every language, so two people whose wallets use different languages can still compare them.",
  "@helpPairWhyP4": {
    "description": "Help › Pairing › Why check the words: the SAS word names are English on purpose, for comparing across languages."
  },
  "helpPairWordsTitle": "Spoken invites",
  "@helpPairWordsTitle": {
    "description": "Topic 3 title."
  },
  "helpPairWordsShort": "Five pictures with names stand in for a link when there is nothing to scan or paste.",
  "@helpPairWordsShort": {
    "description": "Topic 3 lede."
  },
  "helpPairWordsP1": "When one wallet has no camera, or the invite cannot be sent as a link, the inviting wallet shows five pairing words. The other person types their names under **Got words or a link?**; the field completes each name after a few letters. The words are good for 10 minutes.",
  "@helpPairWordsP1": {
    "description": "Topic 3."
  },
  "helpPairWordsP2": "The words only find the invite. Whoever has them is in the same position as someone who received the link, so check the four words together afterwards as usual.",
  "@helpPairWordsP2": {
    "description": "Topic 3."
  },
  "helpPairWordsP3": "Spoken invites need Epicbox. While Epicbox is off, send the invite link instead.",
  "@helpPairWordsP3": {
    "description": "Topic 3."
  },
  "helpPairInvTitle": "Invoices from paired contacts",
  "@helpPairInvTitle": {
    "description": "Topic 4 title."
  },
  "helpPairInvShortDesktop": "Only a Verified contact you allow can ask you to pay. Each invoice waits for the wallet password.",
  "@helpPairInvShortDesktop": {
    "description": "helpPairInvShort on the desktop, which pays with the wallet password, not a PIN."
  },
  "helpPairInvShort": "Only a Verified contact you allow can ask you to pay. Each invoice waits for your PIN.",
  "@helpPairInvShort": {
    "description": "Topic 4 lede."
  },
  "helpPairInvP1": "A shop, an exchange or a person you paired can send invoices, if you allow it: **Allow invoices** on the contact page, or under [[Settings › Invoices]]. The switch works only for a Verified contact.",
  "@helpPairInvP1": {
    "description": "Topic 4."
  },
  "helpPairInvP2Desktop": "An invoice shows under [[Invoices › Pay]] with the contact’s name, the amount and a reference. Nothing is paid until you approve it with the wallet password. An invoice never adds money to your wallet.",
  "@helpPairInvP2Desktop": {
    "description": "helpPairInvP2 on the desktop, which pays with the wallet password, not a PIN."
  },
  "helpPairInvP2": "An invoice shows under [[Invoices › Pay]] with the contact’s name, the amount and a reference. Nothing is paid until you approve it with your PIN. An invoice never adds money to your wallet.",
  "@helpPairInvP2": {
    "description": "Topic 4."
  },
  "helpPairInvP3": "Turning the switch off is immediate: new invoices from that contact are dropped and open ones are declined.",
  "@helpPairInvP3": {
    "description": "Topic 4."
  },
  "helpPairInvP4": "An exchange that pairs with you deposits and withdraws this way. Deposit: the exchange asks, you approve, the exchange posts. Withdrawal: the exchange sends the payment, your wallet answers on its own, the exchange posts.",
  "@helpPairInvP4": {
    "description": "Topic 4."
  },
  "helpPairRestoreTitle": "What a restore does",
  "@helpPairRestoreTitle": {
    "description": "Topic 5 title."
  },
  "helpPairRestoreShort": "Restoring from the recovery phrase brings every pairing back with the wallet data file; without it, re-pairing finds the same keys.",
  "@helpPairRestoreShort": {
    "description": "Topic 5 lede."
  },
  "helpPairRestoreP1": "With the wallet data file, a restore brings every pairing back unchanged: same keys, same Verified marks, same settings. Contacts notice nothing.",
  "@helpPairRestoreP1": {
    "description": "Topic 5."
  },
  "helpPairRestoreP2": "Without the file, the pairings are gone from this wallet, but the keys are not: they come from the recovery phrase. Pair again with a contact who kept the pairing, and their wallet finds the same keys. It tells them the keys are back, and the contact keeps its Verified mark. On this wallet, Seal payments and Allow invoices start at their defaults.",
  "@helpPairRestoreP2": {
    "description": "Help › Pairing › What a restore does, paragraph 2."
  },
  "helpPairRestoreP3": "A contact sees **Keys changed** only when the phrase is a different one. That can mean a new wallet, or someone else using the name. Check the words again before trusting it.",
  "@helpPairRestoreP3": {
    "description": "Topic 5."
  },
  "helpPairRestoreP4": "Use a pairing on one device at a time. Wallet data imported on a second device asks before using the contacts there.",
  "@helpPairRestoreP4": {
    "description": "Topic 5."
  },
